1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:02,443
Dana almost approved that static.

2
00:00:02,443 --> 00:00:07,579
It looked right at a glance: product in frame, right pose, text where text should

3
00:00:07,579 --> 00:00:07,769
be.

4
00:00:07,769 --> 00:00:12,525
Then she zoomed in on the overlay and read it twice before she believed it.

5
00:00:12,525 --> 00:00:17,661
Five of Harlan's twenty statics garbled the mmHg overlay exactly like that: close

6
00:00:17,661 --> 00:00:21,465
enough to pass a fast look, wrong enough to fail a real one.

7
00:00:21,465 --> 00:00:26,602
Section 3.1 already told you why: long text baked into a generated image is still

8
00:00:26,602 --> 00:00:29,772
genuinely unreliable, no matter how good the tool.

9
00:00:29,772 --> 00:00:31,991
So the fix isn't a cleverer prompt.

10
00:00:31,991 --> 00:00:37,254
It's removing the job from the generator entirely for anything longer than a couple

11
00:00:37,254 --> 00:00:37,825
of words.

12
00:00:37,825 --> 00:00:43,088
If you recognise this pattern from earlier in the course, you're right to: it's the

13
00:00:43,088 --> 00:00:48,097
same move as Section 3.3's colour fix, narrowing exactly what you're asking the

14
00:00:48,097 --> 00:00:50,127
generator to be responsible for.

15
00:00:50,127 --> 00:00:55,136
It's worth being honest about why a better prompt genuinely doesn't solve this.

16
00:00:55,136 --> 00:01:00,462
You could spend an hour rewording the instruction, trying "render this text exactly"

17
00:01:00,462 --> 00:01:05,725
or "double-check the spelling before finishing," and the failure rate barely moves,

18
00:01:05,725 --> 00:01:08,769
because the model isn't ignoring an instruction.

19
00:01:08,769 --> 00:01:13,588
It's attempting the same hard task, glyph by glyph, every time, and a longer

20
00:01:13,588 --> 00:01:17,900
sentence is just more chances for one of those glyphs to land wrong.

21
00:01:18,450 --> 00:01:22,472
Here's the actual change, and notice it isn't a new capability.

22
00:01:22,472 --> 00:01:24,770
It's a different division of labour.

23
00:01:24,770 --> 00:01:29,941
Batch two asked the generator to do two jobs at once: make a convincing photo and

24
00:01:29,941 --> 00:01:32,239
typeset an exact sentence inside it.

25
00:01:32,239 --> 00:01:37,411
Batch three asks it to do one job, the photo, and hands the typesetting to a tool

26
00:01:37,411 --> 00:01:38,815
built for typesetting.

27
00:01:38,815 --> 00:01:43,858
Twenty clean images, twenty exact overlays added afterward, zero garbled lines,

28
00:01:43,858 --> 00:01:48,519
because nothing was ever asked to render text it couldn't reliably place.

29
00:01:48,519 --> 00:01:53,434
You'll notice this also makes the overlay itself more flexible, not just more

30
00:01:53,434 --> 00:01:54,009
reliable.

31
00:01:54,009 --> 00:01:59,308
Once your copy lives in a design tool instead of baked into pixels, changing a word

32
00:01:59,308 --> 00:02:01,989
doesn't mean regenerating the whole image.

33
00:02:01,989 --> 00:02:07,288
You edit a text layer and export again, in seconds rather than another trip through

34
00:02:07,288 --> 00:02:08,181
the generator.

35
00:02:08,181 --> 00:02:11,948
That flexibility compounds across a real testing programme.

36
00:02:11,948 --> 00:02:16,928
If Section 6.2 ever flags a claim that needs rewording, or if Section 8's test

37
00:02:16,928 --> 00:02:22,035
surfaces a line that should say something slightly different, a baked-in version

38
00:02:22,035 --> 00:02:27,206
means regenerating the image from scratch and hoping the rest of it comes out the

39
00:02:27,206 --> 00:02:27,525
same.

40
00:02:27,525 --> 00:02:32,824
A separated overlay means changing one line of text and shipping the correction the

41
00:02:32,824 --> 00:02:33,781
same afternoon.

42
00:02:34,331 --> 00:02:38,398
This isn't a blanket rule against any text in a generated image.

43
00:02:38,398 --> 00:02:43,163
A single word or a bare number is short enough that the risk stays low, and

44
00:02:43,163 --> 00:02:48,501
sometimes baking it in genuinely reads more natural than an obvious overlay, sitting

45
00:02:48,501 --> 00:02:52,631
inside the scene rather than sitting on top of it like a sticker.

46
00:02:52,631 --> 00:02:57,841
The line to hold is specific: anything quoting the product truth document exactly,

47
00:02:57,841 --> 00:03:03,179
where a dropped word changes the claim, goes through the generate-clean-then-overlay

48
00:03:03,179 --> 00:03:05,657
workflow every time, without exception.

49
00:03:05,657 --> 00:03:08,389
The mmHg spec is exactly that kind of line.

50
00:03:08,389 --> 00:03:11,248
It's not persuasive copy you can approximate.

51
00:03:11,248 --> 00:03:13,345
It's a fact that has to be exact.

52
00:03:13,345 --> 00:03:18,619
Ask yourself one question per line of copy before you decide where it belongs: if a

53
00:03:18,619 --> 00:03:23,385
single word dropped out silently, would anyone notice, and would it matter?

54
00:03:23,385 --> 00:03:25,164
For "Comfort", probably not.

55
00:03:25,164 --> 00:03:29,103
For "15 to 20 mmHg", it changes what you're claiming entirely.

56
00:03:29,653 --> 00:03:34,561
I want to be honest about what this actually costs, because "generate clean, add

57
00:03:34,561 --> 00:03:37,015
text after" sounds free and isn't quite.

58
00:03:37,015 --> 00:03:42,106
Twenty images, each needing an overlay placed and checked, is real manual time that

59
00:03:42,106 --> 00:03:44,560
a fully automated batch doesn't require.

60
00:03:44,560 --> 00:03:49,345
It's also non-negotiable for anything quoting Harlan's exact spec, because the

61
00:03:49,345 --> 00:03:54,436
alternative is a claim that reads as garbled at best and wrong at worst, reaching a

62
00:03:54,436 --> 00:03:56,092
real customer on a real ad.

63
00:03:56,092 --> 00:03:59,344
Twenty minutes of overlay work is cheap against that.

64
00:03:59,344 --> 00:04:04,312
Build the overlay step into your actual time budget for a batch, the same way you

65
00:04:04,312 --> 00:04:08,116
budgeted twenty minutes for the angle map back in Section 2.6.

66
00:04:08,116 --> 00:04:12,962
Treating it as a rounding error is how a workshop that should take an afternoon

67
00:04:12,962 --> 00:04:14,618
quietly loses its schedule.

68
00:04:15,168 --> 00:04:20,408
Harlan's twenty statics are genuinely clean now: on brand, on spec, and legible.

69
00:04:20,408 --> 00:04:25,713
Both of the failures from Section 3.2 are closed, with two narrow, specific fixes

70
00:04:25,713 --> 00:04:30,560
rather than one vague instruction to "try again." Next lecture is the last

71
00:04:30,560 --> 00:04:35,800
generation technique before the workshop, replacing the parts of a product shoot

72
00:04:35,800 --> 00:04:37,961
this system can actually replace.

73
00:04:37,961 --> 00:04:43,332
Before then, write down that one line of exact copy your own product cannot afford

74
00:04:43,332 --> 00:04:44,184
to get wrong.

75
00:04:44,184 --> 00:04:49,423
That's the line B03 will remind you, every batch, to add afterward, not bake in.
